% The emesis finding. Gus: the Council finds it unsupported — the antiemetic
% is not reasonable and necessary. Hal: nobody has made the finding — the
% answer is conditional on it.
scenario emesis_findings is:
    claim 1 is for the ondansetron.
    the HCPCS code of the ondansetron is "J8597".
    the ondansetron is furnished to Gus.
    claim 1 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the ondansetron is used in conjunction with the capecitabine.
    the ondansetron is administered 1 hours before the oral anticancer drug.
    the HCPCS code of the capecitabine is "J8520".
    the capecitabine is furnished to Gus.
    the diagnosis code of Gus is "C18.9".
    the capecitabine is approved by the FDA.
    the capecitabine is prescribed by a practitioner licensed under state law to prescribe anticancer chemotherapeutic agents.
    the finding anticancer drug likely to induce emesis about Gus is unsupported, according to the Council, because "the record shows no nausea or vomiting on the prior cycles and no prophylactic antiemetic order".
    claim 2 is for the other ondansetron.
    the HCPCS code of the other ondansetron is "J8597".
    the other ondansetron is furnished to Hal.
    claim 2 is for a supply of 1 months.
    the other ondansetron is used in conjunction with the other capecitabine.
    the other ondansetron is administered 1 hours before the oral anticancer drug.
    the HCPCS code of the other capecitabine is "J8520".
    the other capecitabine is furnished to Hal.
    the diagnosis code of Hal is "C18.9".
    the other capecitabine is approved by the FDA.
    the other capecitabine is prescribed by a practitioner licensed under state law to prescribe anticancer chemotherapeutic agents.
    rn expects answers ["claim 2 is reasonable and necessary"] and unknowns ["the finding anticancer drug likely to induce emesis about Hal is established"].
